WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:04.510
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:10.980
This image is the result of a light simulation
program, created by research scientists.

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:17.750
It looks absolutely beautiful, but the light
simulation algorithm is only part of the recipe

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:18.750
here.

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:23.740
To create something like this, we also need
a good artist who can produce high-quality

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:29.980
geometry, lighting, and, of course, good,
life-like material models.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:35.470
For instance, without the materials part,
we would see something like this.

00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:37.680
Not very exciting, right?

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:43.150
Previously, we introduced a technique that
learns our preferences and helps filling these

00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:45.510
scenes with materials.

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:53.920
This work can also generate variants of the
same materials as well.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:01:01.660
In a later technique, we could even take a
sample image, completely destroy it in photoshop,

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:08.630
and our neural networks would find a photorealistic
material that matches these crazy ideas.

00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:12.320
Links to both of these works are available
in the video description.

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:18.750
And, to improve these digital materials, this
new paper introduces something that the authors

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:24.360
call a multi-resolution neural material representation.

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:25.700
What is that?

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:31.030
Well, it is something that is able to put
amazingly complex material models in our light

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:38.230
transport programs, and not only that, but…oh
my.

00:01:38.230 --> 00:01:39.520
Look at that!

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:44.750
We can even zoom in so far that we see the
snagged threads.

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:48.780
That is the magic of the multi-resolution
part of the technique.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:55.000
The neural part means that the technique looks
at lots of measured material reflectance data,

00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:01.450
this is what describes a real-world material,
and compresses this description down into

00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:04.820
a representation that is manageable.

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:06.250
Okay…why?

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:09.099
Well, look.

00:02:09.099 --> 00:02:10.869
Here is a reference material.

00:02:10.869 --> 00:02:17.450
You see, these are absolutely beautiful, no
doubt, but are often prohibitively expensive

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:18.840
to store directly.

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:25.860
This new method introduces these neural materials
to approximate the real world materials, but

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:30.110
in a way that is super cheap to compute and
store.

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:36.430
So, our first question is, how do these neural
materials compare to these real, reference

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:37.430
materials?

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:39.090
What do you think?

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:44.740
How much worse a quality do we have to expect
to be able to use these in our rendering systems?

00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:50.810
Well, you tell me, because you are already
looking at the new technique right now.

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:55.990
I quickly switched from the reference to the
result with new method already.

00:02:55.990 --> 00:02:58.069
How cool is that?

00:02:58.069 --> 00:02:59.069
Look.

00:02:59.069 --> 00:03:07.019
This was the expensive reference material,
and this is fast neural material counterpart

00:03:07.019 --> 00:03:08.250
for it.

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:11.220
So, how hard is this to pull off?

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:15.450
Well, let’s look at some more results side
by side.

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:17.099
Here is the reference.

00:03:17.099 --> 00:03:22.900
And here are two techniques from one and two
years ago that try to approximate it.

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:29.140
And you see that if we zoom in real close,
these fine details are gone.

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:30.820
Do we have to live with that?

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:35.080
Or, maybe, can the new method do better?

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:38.670
Hold on to your papers, and let’s see.

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:39.670
Wow!

00:03:39.670 --> 00:03:46.540
While it is not a 100% perfect, there is absolutely
no contest compared to the previous methods.

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:53.110
It outperforms them handily in every single
case of these complex materials I came across.

00:03:53.110 --> 00:03:57.110
And when I say complex materials, I really
mean it.

00:03:57.110 --> 00:04:03.470
Look at how beautifully it captures not only
the texture of this piece of embroidery, but,

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:06.940
when we move the light source around, oh wow!

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:12.459
Look at the area here around the vertical
black stripe and how its specular reflections

00:04:12.459 --> 00:04:15.090
change with the lighting.

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:21.089
And note that none of these are real images,
all of them come from a computer program.

00:04:21.089 --> 00:04:23.389
This is truly something else.

00:04:23.389 --> 00:04:24.729
Loving it.

00:04:24.729 --> 00:04:28.990
So, if it really works so well, where is the
catch?

00:04:28.990 --> 00:04:32.610
Does it work only on cloth-like materials?

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:34.680
No-no, not in the slightest!

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:43.210
It also works really well on rocks, insulation
foam, even turtle shells and a variety of

00:04:43.210 --> 00:04:44.889
other materials.

00:04:44.889 --> 00:04:49.360
The paper contains a ton more examples than
we can showcase here, so make sure to have

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:51.610
a look in the video description.

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:57.360
I guess this means that it requires a huge
and expensive neural network to pull off,

00:04:57.360 --> 00:04:58.360
right?

00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:00.779
Well, let’s have a look.

00:05:00.779 --> 00:05:03.270
Whoa, now that’s something.

00:05:03.270 --> 00:05:10.319
It does not require a deep and heavy-duty
neural network, just 4 layers are enough.

00:05:10.319 --> 00:05:15.800
And this, by today’s standard, is a lightweight
network that can take these expensive reference

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:21.259
materials and compress them down in a matter
of milliseconds.

00:05:21.259 --> 00:05:24.099
And they almost look the same.

00:05:24.099 --> 00:05:27.599
Materials into our computer simulations straight
from reality?

00:05:27.599 --> 00:05:28.710
Yes please!

00:05:28.710 --> 00:05:35.439
So, from now on, we will get cheaper and better
material models for animation movies, computer

00:05:35.439 --> 00:05:38.770
games, and visualization applications!

00:05:38.770 --> 00:05:40.990
Sign me up right now!

00:05:40.990 --> 00:05:54.110
What a time to 

00:05:54.110 --> 00:06:29.020
be alive!

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:37.509
Thanks 
for watching and for your generous support,

00:06:37.509 --> 00:06:38.719
and I'll see you next time!

